---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/cosmetology/foundational/infection-control-and-salon-safety">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cosmetology's first professional duty is invisible: keeping clients safe from the last client.
    Salons work on skin, nails and hair — often with sharp tools and reactive chemicals — so
    infection control is the licence's foundation, tested before technique on the state board exam.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="clean → disinfect → sterilise, and single-use rules" widget="equationStep" config={levelsCfg} />

  <h2>The organisms that wrote the rules</h2>
  <p>
    Salon infections have names behind them: mycobacterium folliculitis from contaminated pedicure
    baths ("whirlpool footbath" outbreaks), hepatitis B from reused nail implements, fungal
    infections from unsanitised tools. Every disinfection standard descends from a documented
    outbreak — which is why foot spas have their own cleaning regimes with recorded logs.
  </p>

  <h2>Chemistry is the second safety layer</h2>
  <p>
    Hair colour (oxidative dyes), perms (reducing agents), relaxers (strong alkalis) and bleach
    (hydrogen peroxide + persulfate powders) are genuinely reactive chemistry. The rules — patch
    tests, never mixing products, ventilation, gloves — exist because allergic reactions and
    chemical burns are the salon's real emergencies. Know the SDS sheet; it is a legal document
    for every product on the shelf.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the exam tests science first</h2>
  <p>
    The NIC practical assesses technique, but the theory exam opens with microbiology and
    chemistry because those are the public-health layers of the licence. A great haircut that
    spreads infection is a professional failure; the science section is where the licence draws
    that line.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NIC examination content outline; Milady <em>Standard Cosmetology</em>; state board regulations.</p>
</TopicPage>
